<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kinkaku-ji</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Golden Pavilion, Rokuon-ji</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kinkaku-ji, the Temple of the Golden Pavilion, is a Zen temple in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. It began as the retirement villa of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshimitsu">Ashikaga Yoshimitsu</a>, who built the gold-leafed pavilion from 1397; after his death in 1408 it became a temple. The original pavilion was burned down by a monk in 1950 and rebuilt in 1955.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kinkaku-ji.jpg" alt="Kinkaku-ji" width="320" height="480" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nacaru · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Golden_Pavilion_Kinkaku-ji_water_mirror_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
